\begin_layout Title
Aligning NIST Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The term "window" means the time delay between a single local SYNC pulse and its associated local CLICK.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To clear up any confusion and help prune false positives during the cross-matching step break down how the local window operates versus how the joint alignment operates:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Local Time Window (The Filter)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A local "window" is the strictly bounded time delay between a SYNC pulse (Channel 6) and a CLICK (Channel 0) within A's own data stream (and separately,
 within B's stream).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As your histogram verified earlier (3503,
 6013,
 3516...),
 a real,
 physically valid photon click always happens within a very tight number of ticks immediately following its corresponding local reference clock pulse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
How it prunes False Positives:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the printout for A look at item 91 (CLICK at 5486605) following item 89 (SYNC at 5422382).
 The difference is exactly 64,223 ticks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the local window check says a valid click must arrive within 65,000 ticks of a sync then item 91 is a valid local event.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
If a click arrives 120,000 ticks after a sync then it falls outside the window and it should beinstantly discarded as background noise or a false positive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Joint Time Alignment (The Cross-Match)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The cross-matching step does not look at the time between an A click and a B click.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because A's and B's individual laser pulses are emitted simultaneously from the same central source,
 A and B should be matched by aligning their identical global SYNC time-tags.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Looking at the printouts,
 we can see why the "other thread's" sorting algorithm broke the synchronization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Looking at the raw Time_Tag values,
 A's items and B's items are completely misaligned by index number:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Alice's Item 89 is a SYNC at 5422382.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bob's Item 90 is a SYNC at 5680591.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These two records do not represent the same physical trial!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
B's clock has drifted roughly 258,209 ticks ahead of A's item 89.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If cross-matching code blindly links A's Item 89 to B's Item 89 because their list indices match it will pair completely unrelated trials creating a massive wave of false positives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Correct Way to Align Your Data Lists
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Starting again with a small sample size is a brilliant strategy.
 It allows you to step through the records row-by-row to ensure the pointer logic holds before processing millions of events.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To completely eliminate the cross-matching false positives in a small sample the loop should match records purely by their absolute Time_Tag values not by their list indices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example,
 A's Item 92 (SYNC at 5551487) and B's Item 88 (POL_0 at 5551837).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Those timestamps are incredibly close (only 350 ticks apart) which tells us that A's Item 92 and B's Item 88 belong to the same physical trial sequence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When you write the matching loop in Ada,
 use a "sliding pointer" method to keep the global clocks locked together:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Conceptual logic to align the asynchronous lists cleanly
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if Abs (A_Data(A_Index).Time_Tag - B_Data (B_Index).Time_Tag) < Global_Sync_Tolerance then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- This is a confirmed synchronized global trial.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Check the local SYNC-to-CLICK windows for A and B separately.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Process_CHSH_Matrix (A_Data (A_Index),
 B_Data (B_Index));
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
A_Index := A_Index + 1;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
B_Index := B_Index + 1;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
elsif A_Data (A_Index).Time_Tag < B_Data (B_Index).Time_Tag then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- A's clock is lagging behind B's list pointer so advance the A pointer to catch up
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
A_Index := A_Index + 1;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
else  --  B's clock is lagging behind A's list pointer so advance he B pointer to catch up
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
B_Index := B_Index + 1;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By enforcing this absolute timestamp check the pointers will dynamically slide past the "empty" syncs where no clicks occurred,
 perfectly locking A's and B's genuine concurrent trials together.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How is the small sample size looking when you compare the absolute timestamps between Alice and Bob's files manually on your screen?
 Let's get these clocks perfectly synchronized!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To help tailor the exact sliding check for your loop,
 let me know:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Approximately how many time-tag ticks separate an Alice SYNC pulse from a Bob SYNC pulse when they represent the exact same trial?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* What variable type (e.g.,
 Duration,
 Long_Float,
 or custom integer) is your Double_Positive time-tag based on?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Process_CHSH_Matrix routine
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To handle your synchronized event pairs,
 your Process_CHSH_Matrix routine needs to evaluate the active measurement channels for both sides and sort them into a standard 4x4 matrix array.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because your Data_Record lists contain sequentially listed events,
 the routine must look backward or forward slightly from the matched SYNC markers to see which polarization channel (POL_0 or POL_45) was active,
 and whether a valid local CLICK arrived within your successful time-of-flight window.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here is a structurally complete,
 strictly typed Ada example for your matrix processor using native types that easily drop into your VS Code project.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Matrix and Array Definitions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First,
 define a clean,
 bounded index type for the settings and outcomes so your matrix tracking registers are robustly constrained:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
type Setting_Type is (Setting_0,
 Setting_45,
 Unknown_Setting);
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
type Outcome_Type is (Hit,
 No_Hit);
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- A 4x4 matrix representation tracking:
 [Alice_Setting,
 Bob_Setting,
 Alice_Outcome,
 Bob_Outcome]
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
type CHSH_Matrix_Array is array (Setting_Type range Setting_0 ..
 Setting_45,
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Setting_Type range Setting_0 ..
 Setting_45,
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Outcome_Type,
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Outcome_Type) of Long_Integer;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Global or package-level counter matrix initialized cleanly to zero
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CHSH_Matrix :
 CHSH_Matrix_Array := (others => (others => (others => (others => 0))));
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Process_CHSH_Matrix Routine
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This routine takes Alice and Bob's matched SYNC list indices from your main alignment tracker,
 looks at the neighboring records to extract the parameters,
 and increments the matching cell in your CHSH_Matrix register:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
procedure Process_CHSH_Matrix (
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
A_List :
 in Data_List_Type;
 -- Your generated array/vector of Data_Record
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
A_Index :
 in Integer;
 -- Current Alice SYNC list item index
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
B_List :
 in Data_List_Type;
 -- Your generated array/vector of Data_Record
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
B_Index :
 in Integer;
 -- Current Bob SYNC list item index
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Window :
 in Double_Positive -- Your local maximum SYNC-to-CLICK threshold
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
) is
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
Alice_Setting     :
 Setting_Type := Unknown_Setting;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Bob_Setting      :
 Setting_Type := Unknown_Setting;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Alice_Outcome :
 Outcome_Type := No_Hit;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Bob_Outcome   :
 Outcome_Type := No_Hit;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Timing references
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
A_Sync_Time   :
 constant Double_Positive := A_List(A_Index).Time_Tag;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
B_Sync_Time   :
 constant Double_Positive := B_List(B_Index).Time_Tag;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
begin
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- 1.
 EXTRACT ALICE'S PARAMETERS FOR THIS TRIAL
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Evaluate neighboring records to identify which polarizer fired for this Sync
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if A_Index + 1 <= A_List'Last then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
if A_List(A_Index + 1).Channel = POL_0 then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
Alice_Setting := Setting_0;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
elsif A_List(A_Index + 1).Channel = POL_45 then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
Alice_Setting := Setting_45;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Check if a CLICK exists within the tight time-of-flight window boundary
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if A_Index + 2 <= A_List'Last and then A_List(A_Index + 2).Channel = CLICK then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
if A_List(A_Index + 2).Time_Tag - A_Sync_Time <= Window then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
Alice_Outcome := Hit;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- 2.
 EXTRACT BOB'S PARAMETERS FOR THIS TRIAL
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Evaluate neighboring records to identify which polarizer fired for this Sync
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if B_Index + 1 <= B_List'Last then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
if B_List(B_Index + 1).Channel = POL_0 then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
Bob_Setting := Setting_0;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
elsif B_List(B_Index + 1).Channel = POL_45 then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
Bob_Setting := Setting_45;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Check if a CLICK exists within the tight time-of-flight window boundary
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if B_Index + 2 <= B_List'Last and then B_List(B_Index + 2).Channel = CLICK then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
if B_List(B_Index + 2).Time_Tag - B_Sync_Time <= Window then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
Bob_Outcome := Hit;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- 3.
 SORT AND INCREMENT THE MATCHING MATRIX CELL
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Only increment if settings were successfully extracted on both sides
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if Alice_Setting /= Unknown_Setting and Bob_Setting /= Unknown_Setting then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
CHSH_Matrix(Alice_Setting,
 Bob_Setting,
 Alice_Outcome,
 Bob_Outcome) :=
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CHSH_Matrix(Alice_Setting,
 Bob_Setting,
 Alice_Outcome,
 Bob_Outcome) + 1;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end Process_CHSH_Matrix;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
How to Calculate Your Final E(AB) Values
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once the sample loops finish running cleanly parse this 4x4 matrix to compute the actual binary expectation values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To extract the final E(AB) value for a specific setting pair (e.g.,
 Alice at POL_0 and Bob at POL_0) using your strict Kolmogorov-type discrete count formula,
 use this format:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
function Calculate_Expectation (A_Set :
 Setting_Type;
 B_Set :
 Setting_Type) return Long_Float is
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
N_Plus_Plus :
 Long_Integer;
 -- Both recorded a photon hit
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
N_Minus_Minus :
 Long_Integer;
 -- Neither recorded a photon hit (empty trials)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
N_Plus_Minus :
 Long_Integer;
 -- Alice hit,
 Bob missed
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
N_Minus_Plus :
 Long_Integer;
 -- Alice missed,
 Bob hit
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Total_Trials :
 Long_Integer;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
begin
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
N_Plus_Plus := CHSH_Matrix(A_Set,
 B_Set,
 Hit,
 Hit);
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
N_Minus_Minus := CHSH_Matrix(A_Set,
 B_Set,
 No_Hit,
 No_Hit);
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
N_Plus_Minus := CHSH_Matrix(A_Set,
 B_Set,
 Hit,
 No_Hit);
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
N_Minus_Plus := CHSH_Matrix(A_Set,
 B_Set,
 No_Hit,
 Hit);
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Total_Trials := N_Plus_Plus + N_Minus_Minus + N_Plus_Minus + N_Minus_Plus;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if Total_Trials = 0 then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
return 0.0;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
--  Discrete binary expectation formula:
 (Parity Matches - Parity Mismatches) / Total
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
return Long_Float(N_Plus_Plus + N_Minus_Minus - N_Plus_Minus - N_Minus_Plus) / Long_Float(Total_Trials);
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
end Calculate_Expectation;
\end_layout

